I wouldn't do the "less cautious" part. But skipping conducts to make
your games faster is fine, since conducts are mainly either if you can
handle and ascend quite any game and if you want some thrill or new
challenging situations (modulo the fact that even for conduct games
there are recipes), or if you want a harder game to train some specific
skill (e.g. how to survive if you don't find food) without the wish or
necessity to ascend. But the mentioned conducts to not use wishes and
polypiles are no "real" conducts, IMO, it depends on what you find and
those actions may be unnecessary in a game, without you even noticing
that you comply with the conduct.

> Also very interesting was how Marvin handled the levels under the
> castle.  He skipped a lot of the mazes and fought his way through with
> a silver dagger!  I guess you don't need a silver saber or an artifact
> with cold or lightning damage in the end game.

When you have The Amulet you want to be fast, avoid any unnecessary
combat, so even the tin-opener as a weapon might be okay. But it is
still helpful to have some efficient means to dispatch monsters. The
silver dagger is not that bad if used at Expert proficiency against
silver hating monsters like demons and vampires. (I don't know what
specific game you have seen, but you would want to add attack spells
to your tactics repertoire, and teleporting wonsters out of your way,
and jumping/teleporting around monsters.)

